	"nodes":[
		{"id":"3c5f82f975b75276","type":"file","file":"Agent/GoEx/NOTE.md","x":-3200,"y":-380,"width":1007,"height":520},
		{"id":"3a9d496a35b82298","type":"text","text":"post-facto LLM validation","x":-1376,"y":-990,"width":267,"height":60},
		{"id":"7838d74fd4cb7b3b","type":"file","file":"Agent/GoEx/Figure2.png","x":1300,"y":-4240,"width":840,"height":708},
		{"id":"82d2ea4d758ef6e0","type":"text","text":"Figure 2: GoEx’s runtime for executing RESTful API calls. Upon receiving the user’s prompt, GoEx presents two alternatives. First, an LLM can be prompted to come up with the (Action, Undo-Action) pair. Second, the application developer can provide tuples of actions and their corresponding undo-actions (function calls) from which the LLM can pick amongst.","x":1384,"y":-3520,"width":672,"height":154},
		{"id":"833c66128bcc06e5","type":"file","file":"Agent/GoEx/Figure3.png","x":1300,"y":-3060,"width":845,"height":560},
		{"id":"a4666a04368bd534","type":"text","text":"Figure 3:Runtime for executing actions on a database. We present two techniques to determine if a proposed action can be undone. On the left, for non-transactional databases like MongoDB, and for flexibility, we prompt the LLM to generate (Action, Undo-Action, test-bed) tuples, which we then evaluate in a isolated container to catch any false (Action, Undo-Action) pairs. On the right, we can provide a deterministic undo with guarantees by employing the transaction semantics of databases.","x":1337,"y":-2460,"width":771,"height":178},
		{"id":"ace8e5bc5eb6a573","type":"text","text":"eg2: Database Operations","x":344,"y":-2710,"width":280,"height":60},
		{"id":"776b523968255c0c","type":"text","text":"examples","x":-240,"y":-2700,"width":146,"height":50},
		{"id":"715409981df62cf5","type":"text","text":"eg1: RESTful API calls","x":467,"y":-3443,"width":250,"height":50},
		{"id":"08b5eb4442c374d1","type":"file","file":"Agent/GoEx/Figure4.png","x":1300,"y":-2100,"width":845,"height":582},
		{"id":"060232e4e96fcb18","type":"text","text":"Figure 4:Runtime for executing actions on a filesystem. GoEx presents two abstractions. On the left, the LLM is prompted to come up with an (Action, Undo-Action, test-bed) which GoEx evaluates in a isolated container to catch any false (Action, Undo-Action) pairs. On the right presents deterministic guarantees by using versioning control system like Git or Git LFS.","x":1411,"y":-1480,"width":622,"height":180},
		{"id":"a5eddc96b4240391","type":"text","text":"eg3: File Systems","x":409,"y":-1900,"width":215,"height":60},
		{"id":"df8d7803aec9305c","type":"text","text":"pre-facto LLM validation","x":-1492,"y":1260,"width":250,"height":60}
	],
